;;;171    /*
;;;172     * The head of a delivered frame, over the UART, plus the guard word in front of it.
;;;173     *
;;;174     * This exists because "seq 1, 38400 B" looks the same whether the DMA wrote a frame or
;;;175     * wrote nothing: the byte count comes from the descriptor, not from the data. A dozen
;;;176     * words settle it immediately, and name the failure while doing so:
;;;177     *
;;;178     *   DEADBEEF repeated  - this demo's poison. The DMA never wrote here.
;;;179     *   A5A5A5A5 in [-1]   - the guard is intact, so nothing overran into it.
;;;180     *   one constant value - something wrote, but not pixels (bus stuck, or a sensor
;;;181     *                        putting out a flat field).
;;;182     *   varying values     - real data; compare against the expected format.
;;;183     */
