;;;7      /*
;;;8       * DCI demo: interrupt counting and per-frame reporting.
;;;9       *
;;;10      * Five interrupt sources, three of them always on and two optional:
;;;11      *
;;;12      *   FRAME  always enabled - the driver needs it to close a frame. Where the per-frame
;;;13      *          report is emitted, and where "keep frame N" is decided.
;;;14      *   OVR    always enabled. Logs unconditionally.
;;;15      *   ERR    always enabled. Logs unconditionally.
;;;16      *   VSYNC  off unless DCI_DEMO_IRQ_VSYNC. Counted cumulatively.
;;;17      *   LINE   off unless DCI_DEMO_IRQ_LINE. Counted per frame and cumulatively.
;;;18      *
;;;19      * "Off" here means masked in IER, not counted-and-discarded. The driver enables IT_VSYNC
;;;20      * and IT_LINE if and only if the matching callback pointer in DCI_DMA_config is non-NULL
;;;21      * (dci_int_config() in rtl_dci.c), so leaving the pointer NULL is the register-level
;;;22      * disable. That is why the macros gate a pointer here rather than an IER write: the
;;;23      * driver is not modified, and there is no window in which the interrupt is enabled but
;;;24      * unhandled.
;;;25      *
;;;26      * Ordering constraint worth stating because it is not obvious and it fails silently:
;;;27      * DCI_Capture_Start() calls dci_int_config(), which ASSIGNS IER, immediately before
;;;28      * enabling the peripheral. Any IER bit set with DCI_INTConfig() beforehand is wiped, and
;;;29      * any set afterwards races the first frame. Supplying the callbacks and letting the
;;;30      * driver derive IER is the only approach that is correct at both ends.
;;;31      *
;;;32      * No printing in an ISR except on the error paths. LINE is roughly 28,000 interrupts per
;;;33      * second at 466 lines and 60 fps; a print there does not slow the report down, it causes
;;;34      * the overrun the report would have described. The handlers accumulate into plain
;;;35      * counters and the frame handler emits one line.
;;;36      */
;;;37     
